00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:36.000
Canora and neighboring towns west of Ontario's 90th meridian will drop the twice-yearly

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.920
clock change, global news reports.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.300
The provincial government announced Tuesday that those communities will stay on daylight

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.200
time all year.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.240
Dryden, Sue, look out and pickle Lake already keeps central daylight time, the same zone

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.160
Manitoba uses.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.720
On November 1st, the clock will match in Manitoba and across all of Ontario.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.380
When much of Ontario springs forward an hour in March, the Northwest and Manitoba

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.180
will not move.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.540
Canora Rainy River MPP Greg Rickford said in a Tuesday release that the shift followed

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.240
what he called unanimous consensus.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:11.160
He said that agreement was built with communities and first nations across the Northwest, through

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.480
what he called detailed and nuanced considerations.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:15.940
Several communities surveyed residents.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.220
In Canora, about 6,000 of roughly 16,000 residents answered.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.200
Mayor Andrew Porrier said 93% wanted to keep daylight time.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.880
He said Council passed a resolution to line up with Manitoba, and neighboring communities

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:29.420
did the same.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.460
He said Canora is tied far more to Winnipeg than to the rest of Ontario.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.840
Travel between them, he said, works like Highway 401 for business, tourism, and visits.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.880
That shared clock also covers medical appointments and tourist bookings.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.020
Scott Green, owner and operator of Lake of the Woods Marina and Service Center, said Wednesday

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.700
he wants the match with Winnipeg and Manitoba to hold.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.760
His marinas restaurant and bar hosts watch parties for the Winnipeg Jets.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.260
He said a permanent match makes the game's lineup better and makes a trip to Winnipeg for

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.020
a game easier.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:05.400
Shared time, he said, will also smooth boat taxi and tour bookings, and Zoom calls with

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.520
people in Toronto, because callers there have had to check which clock Canora was on.

00:03:10.588 --> 00:03:14.348
Northwestern Ontario is not the only pocket off its province's main clock.

00:03:14.948 --> 00:03:17.568
British Columbia's Cootney region sits on mountain time.

00:03:18.068 --> 00:03:22.528
Nunavik and Quebec's lower north shore sit on Atlantic time, as do parts of Labrador,

00:03:22.988 --> 00:03:25.528
unlike the rest of Newfoundland and Southeastern Labrador.

00:03:25.948 --> 00:03:30.588
In Golden, British Columbia, Councillor Wesley Ratley said Council last month had planned

00:03:30.588 --> 00:03:33.388
a permanent move to mountain standard time on November 1.

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:38.288
Council then reversed after hundreds of residents asked to stay aligned with the regional district

00:03:38.288 --> 00:03:42.108
of East Cootney to the south, and with other towns on mountain daylight time.

00:03:42.608 --> 00:03:45.428
A large share of Golden's visitors come from Alberta, he said,

00:03:45.728 --> 00:03:48.268
so that alignment could cut confusion for people visiting.

00:03:48.868 --> 00:03:53.108
British Columbia, Alberta, and Manitoba have each said they will remain on daylight time

00:03:53.108 --> 00:03:57.288
starting November 1st. Saskatchewan has kept central standard time all year.

00:03:57.808 --> 00:04:01.168
The other provinces have said in recent months that no change is expected,

00:04:01.548 --> 00:04:03.128
though talks and studies continue.

