00:04:42.589 --> 00:04:47.349
In Montreal, with the wider world in view, Canadian foreign policy and trade experts are

00:04:47.349 --> 00:04:52.349
urging Prime Minister Mark Carney and European Commission President Ursula Von Der Lyon to

00:04:52.349 --> 00:04:56.349
launch a middle-power charter at their late October summit, the Globe and Mail Reports.

00:04:56.829 --> 00:05:00.869
Canada and the European Union meet their later this month to map what the summit calls

00:05:00.869 --> 00:05:02.069
and alliance for the future.

00:05:02.489 --> 00:05:06.889
The aim is closer ties while both try to rely less on the United States under Donald Trump,

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:09.989
described in that report as protectionist and unpredictable.

00:05:10.449 --> 00:05:14.289
The expert group on Canada-US relations published the proposal Thursday.

00:05:14.749 --> 00:05:19.349
The charter would be open to other democracies and patterned on the 1941 Atlantic Charter

00:05:19.349 --> 00:05:21.569
between Franklin Roosevelt and Winston Churchill.

00:05:21.989 --> 00:05:25.929
That statement helped lay groundwork for the United Nations and the North Atlantic Treaty

00:05:25.929 --> 00:05:26.709
Organization.

00:05:27.229 --> 00:05:32.069
Carney popularized middle-power cooperation in a January speech at the World Economic Forum

00:05:32.069 --> 00:05:32.789
in Davos.

00:05:32.789 --> 00:05:37.549
There, he argued the post-Cold War rules-based order has suffered what he called a rupture,

00:05:37.949 --> 00:05:38.769
not a transition.

00:05:39.189 --> 00:05:43.609
He argued middle power such as Canada must move as a group or else end up on the menu.

00:05:44.189 --> 00:05:47.109
The Montreal Summit is aimed at deeper trade ties in cooperation.

00:05:47.749 --> 00:05:52.209
The expert group says Ottawa and Brussels should go past a deal-by-deal approach and write

00:05:52.209 --> 00:05:56.169
a shared political and economic purpose, a middle-power compact for today.

00:05:56.629 --> 00:06:01.509
The authors include former federal cabinet minister Paran Bady, one-time ambassador Jonathan

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:06.749
and freed, former trade negotiator John Weeks, and Brian Klaus, a former deputy chief

00:06:06.749 --> 00:06:08.029
of staff to the Prime Minister.

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:12.589
They argue that if Canada wants substantive relations with other middle powers, it

00:06:12.589 --> 00:06:15.169
has to set out principles that would found that cooperation.

00:06:15.689 --> 00:06:18.269
The 1941 statement had no formal status.

00:06:18.729 --> 00:06:21.889
It said Britain and the United States sought no territorial gains.

00:06:22.269 --> 00:06:25.849
It opposed territorial changes made against the wishes of the people's concerned.

00:06:26.349 --> 00:06:29.009
It backed every country's right to choose its own government.

00:06:29.009 --> 00:06:34.009
The eight-point text also pledged for your trade and economic cooperation, freedom of

00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:37.989
the seas, freedom from fear and want, and the disarmament of aggressor countries.

00:06:38.469 --> 00:06:42.929
It became a founding plan for the post-war order, the United Nations, the Bretton Woods

00:06:42.929 --> 00:06:45.109
system, and the Alliance anchored by NATO.

00:06:45.669 --> 00:06:48.469
The expert group says that order has been severely compromised.

00:06:48.929 --> 00:06:53.669
It says the United States, a signatory, has stopped following rules Washington itself wrote

00:06:53.669 --> 00:06:54.149
in bargain.

00:06:54.149 --> 00:06:58.889
It says Washington has turned commerce built across eight decades of interdependence into

00:06:58.889 --> 00:07:02.709
a lever for economic gain, even when that harms economies worldwide.

00:07:03.109 --> 00:07:07.229
The group says a new charter should restate the Atlantic charter and add five principles

00:07:07.229 --> 00:07:08.329
for a digital age.

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:13.469
In that age, it says, defending democracy depends on the integrity of information systems,

00:07:13.869 --> 00:07:16.989
data and digital infrastructure as much as on military strength.

00:07:17.369 --> 00:07:22.449
Canada and the European Union should pledge, the group says, that technology will not undercut

00:07:22.449 --> 00:07:27.589
human dignity, human security, and democratic self-government, or threaten humanity's survival.

00:07:28.109 --> 00:07:32.949
The principles, as laid out, start with defending democracy at home through free and fair elections,

00:07:33.449 --> 00:07:38.749
peaceful transfer of power, freedom of expression, a free press, and protection of minority rights.

00:07:39.229 --> 00:07:43.709
Second, every country must be able to set its future free from force or coercion,

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:47.569
including a pledge against interference in other country's democratic processes,

00:07:48.089 --> 00:07:51.249
and joint work against disinformation and anti-democratic movements.

00:07:51.249 --> 00:07:56.889
Third, middle powers should reduce weak points in critical supply chains, financial services,

00:07:57.409 --> 00:08:02.209
energy, technology, infrastructure and defense industries, and pledge not to use trade

00:08:02.209 --> 00:08:06.789
as a tool of coercion, while each country keeps the sovereign right to choose its own economic

00:08:06.789 --> 00:08:07.809
or security partners.

00:08:08.369 --> 00:08:13.069
Fourth, they would combine strength through joint investment, production and shared capabilities

00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:18.529
in defense, critical technologies, energy, research and infrastructure, at a scale meant

00:08:18.529 --> 00:08:19.429
to deter aggression.

00:08:19.429 --> 00:08:24.809
The proposal is a paper from the expert group, not a text-carnie or Vonder Lyon have adopted.

